{%- doc -%}
  A product name with the shop's internal codes taken out of it.

  Some names carry a supplier code on the end — `12 PACK GIFT BOX RD` — which the
  Treat House team reads in the admin and a shopper has no use for. The code stays
  on the product, because the team is the reason it is there; this takes it off the
  name on the way to the page.

  Which words go is `settings.title_hidden_words`, a comma-separated list in Theme
  settings, so a code can be added or dropped without a developer.

  **A whole word, not a substring.** The name is split on spaces and a part is
  dropped only when the whole of it matches, ignoring case. That is what keeps
  `Birthday` and `Chocolate` intact while `RD` goes — a substring rule would eat
  the middle of half the catalogue.

  **This is a curtain, not a rename.** The name still reaches checkout, order
  confirmations and invoices, the Google Shopping feed, `products.json`, the
  Storefront API and anything Shopify sends by email, because none of those render
  through the theme. It buys time while the merchant decides whether the products
  can be renamed for real; it is not the same thing as renaming them.

  @param {string} title - The name to clean.

  @example
  {%- capture shown -%}{% render 'strip-hidden-words', title: product.title %}{%- endcapture -%}
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign hidden_words = settings.title_hidden_words | strip
  assign shown = title

  # Nothing configured is the normal case, and it should cost nothing — the name
  # is handed straight back rather than walked word by word.
  if hidden_words != blank and title != blank
    # The list becomes one delimited string matched with `contains`, because Liquid
    # has no way to ask whether an array holds a value.
    assign drop = '|'
    assign configured = hidden_words | split: ','
    for word in configured
      assign word_key = word | strip | downcase
      if word_key != blank
        assign drop = drop | append: word_key | append: '|'
      endif
    endfor

    if drop != '|'
      assign kept = ''
      assign parts = title | split: ' '
      for part in parts
        assign part_key = part | strip | downcase
        if part_key != blank
          assign lookup = '|' | append: part_key | append: '|'
          unless drop contains lookup
            assign kept = kept | append: ' ' | append: part
          endunless
        endif
      endfor

      # A name that was nothing but hidden words would come back empty and leave
      # the card with no heading at all, so there the original stands.
      assign kept = kept | strip
      if kept != blank
        assign shown = kept
      endif
    endif
  endif

  echo shown
-%}
